When a box is restyled in place, Ladybird gives it back the display
its box uses, so a replaced element whose display computes to a
layout-internal value such as table-cell stays an inline box, as
section 2.4 of CSS Display 3 says. Chrome 158, Firefox 151 and WebKit
26.5 keep the size of every element in the test (an input, an img in
table-cell, table-row and table displays, and a canvas, each after a
float) across a restyle of the whole document, as Ladybird now does.
None of the three turns such an element into a table part to begin
with: Blink’s HTMLImageElement::CreateLayoutObject() makes a LayoutImage
whatever the display, Gecko’s nsCSSFrameConstructor::FindHTMLData()
finds an img’s frame data (FindImgData()) ahead of any data for its
display, and WebKit’s HTMLImageElement::createElementRenderer() always
makes a RenderImage.
